---
import Landing from "../layouts/Landing.astro";

const repo = "https://github.com/caioricciuti/ldf";

// Every figure below is copied from docs/reports/2026-10-17-baseline
// (tokens-medium-2026-10-18.md and the "Size step" sections). o200k_base
// tokens, ratio to compact JSON, first 1 000 records of each dataset.
const tokenRows = [
  ["flat_users", "0.61", "0.65", "0.60"],
  ["nested_orders", "0.64", "1.08", "n/a"],
  ["sparse_events", "0.80", "1.22", "0.61, lossy"],
  ["wide_table", "0.43", "0.44", "0.43"],
  ["empty_values", "0.36", "1.27", "n/a"],
  ["deep_nesting", "0.80", "1.82", "n/a"],
];

const example = `@records order_id:s,customer.id:i,customer.name:s,items:t,total:f,status:s,note:s?
ORD-000001,4654,Val Thompson,2,715.94,shipped,context compare
ORD-000002,50,Sol Ritchie,1,2757.03,cancelled,null
@records.items order_id:s,sku:s,qty:i,price:f
ORD-000001,E5,3,28.78
ORD-000001,C3,4,157.4
ORD-000002,A1,1,2757.03`;

const json = `[{"order_id":"ORD-000001","customer":{"id":4654,"name":"Val Thompson"},
  "items":[{"sku":"E5","qty":3,"price":28.78},{"sku":"C3","qty":4,"price":157.4}],
  "total":715.94,"status":"shipped","note":"context compare"},
 {"order_id":"ORD-000002","customer":{"id":50,"name":"Sol Ritchie"},
  "items":[{"sku":"A1","qty":1,"price":2757.03}],
  "total":2757.03,"status":"cancelled","note":null}]`;

const mcp = `claude mcp add clickhouse -- ldf-mcp clickhouse --url http://127.0.0.1:8123
claude mcp add my-data   -- ldf-mcp proxy -- <any stdio MCP server> [its args]`;
---

<Landing
  title="LDF: tables a language model can read"
  description="Language Data Format: a lossless, line-oriented text encoding of JSON records that language models read for 35 to 45 percent fewer tokens than JSON. Measured, not assumed."
>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="container hero-grid">
      <div class="hero-copy" data-reveal>
        <p class="eyebrow">Language Data Format, v0.1</p>
        <h1 class="h1">Tables a language model can read.</h1>
        <p class="lead">
          LDF turns JSON records into typed tables: one header, one line per record, child tables for
          nested arrays. Nothing is lost on the way in or out, and a frontier model reads it as well as
          JSON for 35 to 45 percent fewer tokens.
        </p>
        <div class="cta">
          <a class="btn btn-light" href="/docs/">Read the docs <span class="arrow">→</span></a>
          <a class="btn btn-ghost" href={repo}>Source on GitHub</a>
        </div>
        <ul class="facts">
          <li><strong>Lossless.</strong> Byte-exact round trip on ten of twelve benchmark datasets; the two sparse ones come back with keys reordered, nothing lost.</li>
          <li><strong>Typed.</strong> Integers, floats, strings, booleans, JSON cells and child tables, declared once in the header.</li>
          <li><strong>Measured.</strong> Four models, three sizes, ten formats. The misses are in the report, not hidden.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="hero-sample" data-reveal>
        <div class="sample">
          <div class="sample-bar"><span>orders.ldf</span><span class="dim">2 records, 1 child table</span></div>
          <pre><code>{example}</code></pre>
        </div>
        <div class="sample faded">
          <div class="sample-bar"><span>the same data as JSON</span><span class="dim">1.6x the tokens</span></div>
          <pre><code>{json}</code></pre>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="format">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head" data-reveal>
        <p class="eyebrow">The format</p>
        <h2 class="h2">Seven rules. <span class="soft">That is the whole spec.</span></h2>
        <p class="lead">A document is a sequence of tables. Each table is a header line and rows. Everything else follows from the data.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rules" data-reveal>
        <div class="rule"><code>@records a:i,b:s</code><p>A header names the table and types each column: <code>i</code> integer, <code>f</code> float, <code>s</code> string, <code>b</code> boolean, <code>j</code> any JSON, <code>t</code> child table. <code>?</code> marks a column that may be null or absent.</p></div>
        <div class="rule"><code>1,hello</code><p>A row is one record, cells separated by commas. Strings are bare unless they need quoting, then they are JSON strings.</p></div>
        <div class="rule"><code>,</code> and <code>null</code><p>An empty cell means the key is absent. <code>null</code> means it is present and null. JSON's distinction survives.</p></div>
        <div class="rule"><code>customer.name:s</code><p>Objects with a stable shape flatten into dotted columns. Irregular ones stay as a JSON cell.</p></div>
        <div class="rule"><code>@records.items</code><p>Arrays of objects become a child table joined on the parent's first column. The parent keeps a count.</p></div>
        <div class="rule"><code>~:j?</code><p>Keys present in fewer than half the rows travel in a small per-row object instead of a mostly empty column. Measured to matter for smaller models.</p></div>
        <div class="rule"><code>1.0</code> stays <code>1.0</code><p>Numbers keep their kind and shortest round-trip text. Integers above 2^53 do not get mangled.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="numbers">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head" data-reveal>
        <p class="eyebrow">Numbers</p>
        <h2 class="h2">Tokens, as a share of compact JSON.</h2>
        <p class="lead">
          o200k_base tokens on the first 1 000 records of the benchmark datasets, from the report's own
          tables. CSV ties LDF where CSV works at all; CSV cannot hold nested data and drops the
          difference between missing and null.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="table-wrap" data-reveal>
        <table class="numbers">
          <thead><tr><th>dataset</th><th>LDF</th><th>TOON</th><th>CSV</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {tokenRows.map(([name, ldf, toon, csv]) => (
              <tr><td><code>{name}</code></td><td class="strong">{ldf}</td><td>{toon}</td><td>{csv}</td></tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div class="stats" data-reveal>
        <div class="stat"><span class="stat-n">131 / 150</span><span class="stat-l">Claude Opus 5.5 at 200 records, zero wrong answers in LDF, the same score as every other format. The misses are an output cap on sums.</span></div>
        <div class="stat"><span class="stat-n">53 / 53</span><span class="stat-l">GPT-6.1 Sol on LDF at 50 records, zero-shot: the prompt names the format and shows the data, nothing more.</span></div>
        <div class="stat"><span class="stat-n">17 / 18</span><span class="stat-l">Claude Haiku 5.5 on 200 sparse events after the v0.1 sparse-key rule, JSON's score at 25 percent fewer tokens. Before the rule it was 13.</span></div>
      </div>
      <p class="note" data-reveal>Below the frontier tier no format is reliably better than another, and the report says so. Read the <a href="/docs/methodology/">methodology</a> before quoting any number.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="mcp">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head" data-reveal>
        <p class="eyebrow">The middle man</p>
        <h2 class="h2">Nobody has to adopt a format. <span class="soft">Put it between the data and the model.</span></h2>
        <p class="lead">
          <code>ldf-mcp</code> is an MCP server. In proxy mode it wraps any stdio MCP server you already use,
          forwards every message, and re-encodes JSON tool results as LDF when that is shorter. The model
          keeps calling the same tools and receives cheaper data. In ClickHouse mode it is the server:
          read-only SQL over the HTTP interface, rows returned as LDF.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="sample wide" data-reveal>
        <div class="sample-bar"><span>Claude Code</span><span class="dim">one line per server</span></div>
        <pre><code>{mcp}</code></pre>
      </div>
      <div class="mcp-points" data-reveal>
        <div><strong>Primer once.</strong> The initialize result carries a short description of the format, so the model has read it before it sees any data.</div>
        <div><strong>Only what helps.</strong> Results under 1 KB, error results and non-text blocks pass through untouched.</div>
        <div><strong>A/B in the same session.</strong> A passthrough flag forwards JSON unchanged, for comparing answers and cost.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="status">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head" data-reveal>
        <p class="eyebrow">Status</p>
        <h2 class="h2">A v0.1 prototype with its evidence attached.</h2>
        <p class="lead">
          One Rust workspace: the format, ten baseline formats implemented faithfully (TOON passes its
          official conformance suite), a benchmark harness, an evaluation harness and the MCP server.
          Every dependency pinned, no network calls outside the MCP server's own query.
        </p>
        <div class="cta">
          <a class="btn btn-light" href="/docs/format/">The format in five minutes <span class="arrow">→</span></a>
          <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/docs/roadmap/">What comes next</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Landing>

<style>
  .hero {
    padding: calc(7rem + env(safe-area-inset-top, 0px)) 0 clamp(3rem, 7vw, 5rem);
    background: radial-gradient(60% 50% at 70% 0%, var(--mint-glow), transparent 70%);
  }
  .hero-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.05fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 4rem);
    align-items: start;
  }
  .h1 {
    font-family: var(--display);
    font-size: clamp(2.4rem, 5.6vw, 4.2rem);
    line-height: 1.02;
    letter-spacing: -0.04em;
    font-weight: 680;
    margin: 1rem 0 0;
    text-wrap: balance;
  }
  .cta {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.75rem;
    margin-top: 2rem;
  }
  .facts {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 2.5rem 0 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.8rem;
    color: var(--text-2);
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    max-width: 34rem;
  }
  .facts strong {
    color: var(--text);
  }
  .hero-sample {
    display: grid;
    gap: 1rem;
    min-width: 0;
  }
  .sample {
    border: 1px solid var(--line-2);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    background: var(--bg-2);
    overflow: hidden;
    min-width: 0;
  }
  .sample.faded {
    opacity: 0.72;
  }
  .sample.wide {
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
  }
  .sample-bar {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 1rem;
    padding: 0.6rem 1rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    color: var(--text-2);
  }
  .sample-bar .dim {
    color: var(--dim);
  }
  .sample pre {
    margin: 0;
    padding: 1rem;
    overflow-x: auto;
    font-size: 0.82rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--text);
  }
  .sample code {
    white-space: pre;
  }
  .rules {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(17rem, 1fr));
    gap: 1rem;
    margin-top: 3rem;
  }
  .rule {
    padding: 1.25rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    background: var(--bg-1);
  }
  .rule > code {
    display: inline-block;
    padding: 0.2rem 0.5rem;
    border-radius: 6px;
    background: var(--bg-2);
    border: 1px solid var(--line-2);
    color: var(--mint);
    font-size: 0.85rem;
  }
  .rule p {
    margin: 0.8rem 0 0;
    color: var(--text-2);
    font-size: 0.93rem;
  }
  .rule p code {
    color: var(--text);
    font-size: 0.85em;
  }
  .table-wrap {
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
    overflow-x: auto;
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    background: var(--bg-1);
  }
  .numbers {
    width: 100%;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
  }
  .numbers th,
  .numbers td {
    text-align: left;
    padding: 0.8rem 1.1rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
  .numbers th {
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--dim);
    font-weight: 600;
  }
  .numbers tbody tr:last-child td {
    border-bottom: 0;
  }
  .numbers .strong {
    color: var(--mint);
    font-weight: 600;
  }
  .stats {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(16rem, 1fr));
    gap: 1rem;
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }
  .stat {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    padding: 1.25rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    background: var(--bg-1);
  }
  .stat-n {
    font-family: var(--display);
    font-size: 2rem;
    letter-spacing: -0.03em;
    font-weight: 650;
  }
  .stat-l {
    color: var(--text-2);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }
  .note {
    margin: 1.5rem 0 0;
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }
  .note a {
    color: var(--text);
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-decoration-color: var(--line-2);
  }
  .mcp-points {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(16rem, 1fr));
    gap: 1rem;
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
    color: var(--text-2);
    font-size: 0.93rem;
  }
  .mcp-points strong {
    color: var(--text);
  }
  .lead code {
    font-size: 0.9em;
    color: var(--text);
  }
  @media (max-width: 56rem) {
    .hero-grid {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
</style>
